1.1.2.m:
"Bestow an embryo, O fathers, / a boy wearing a lotus garland;

so that there may be a male here, / so bring it about, O steam-drinkers!"

With [these verses] the wife should eat the middle ball.

1.1.3.m:
'Be united, O righteous ones, / most full of sweetness with the wave;

uniting milk with milk, / pleasant for the winning of wealth'—

with this [mantra] he pours the water used for rinsing the milking vessel into the pot.

1.2.4.m:
“With the broom they found the altar, the earth; / that earth spread out for the earthly one;

It bears the embryo within the creatures; / from it the sacrifice is extended perpetually.”

With this mantra he sweeps the altar towards the east three times with the broom.

“Men, make this altar now, / acceptable to the gods, in the lap of Aditi;

All the gods have been pleased with this; / may they enter the sacrificer for increase of wealth.”

With this mantra he makes it depressed in the middle, sloping towards the north-east, smooth, and well made; with its shoulders it encompasses the āhavanīya fire, with its hips the gārhapatya fire; with earth brought from elsewhere for one desiring cattle.

1.4.3.m:
'I send you forth to the ocean; / go to your own source;

May our heroes be unharmed; / let not my milk be poured away.'

With this [formula] he accompanies [the waters] that have been poured out.

1.5.2.m:
"I unite you, your hearts; / let your mind be united;

let your breath be united; / let your bodies be united."

With this [mantra], he mixes the materials together.

1.6.1.m:
'Being taken up, deliver me from evil, / which I have committed unknowingly and which knowingly;

whatever sin and evil has been done by day, / from all that, being taken up, release me!'

With this he carries it forward. In the morning [he says]: '...whatever [sin and evil has been done] by night...'

'I offer the immortal offering into the immortal [fire], / Agni into the earth, for the winning of immortality;

Through you, O Agni, I win my desire, / which Prajāpati first won. / Agni into Agni, svāhā!'

With this he deposits it in the evening; in the morning [he says]: 'Sūrya into Agni...'
